---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CtaBand from "../components/CtaBand.astro";
import LineIcon from "../components/LineIcon.astro";
import { SITE } from "../lib/site";

const org = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Organization",
  "@id": SITE.url,
  name: "Alluvium",
  url: SITE.url,
  description: "Power BI and analytics delivery for mid-sized companies. Conversational analytics means asking a certified semantic model.",
};
const website = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebSite",
  "@id": SITE.url + "/",
  name: "Alluvium",
  url: SITE.url,
  description: SITE.description,
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Microsoft Power BI consulting | Alluvium"
  description="Power BI consulting for mid-sized companies. Trusted numbers in the meeting, not more dashboards. Quickstart, models, training, and advisory. Book a Session."
  canonicalPath="/"
  jsonLd={[org, website]}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>Microsoft Power BI consulting</h1>
      <p class="lede">Alluvium is Power BI consulting for mid-sized companies. We automate the report you already rebuild by hand, and we put the numbers on a model finance and operations can both stand behind.</p>
      <p class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/contact">Book a Session</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/power-bi-quickstart">Power BI Quickstart</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-alt" aria-labelledby="principles">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 id="principles">Trusted Power BI models for mid-sized companies</h2>
      <p class="lede">When that model is trusted, people can ask it a question and get the same figure the meeting already uses. That is not a chatbot project.</p>
      <p>
        We do not add dashboards so a slide can say the program is busy. If one report is the bottleneck, start with a
        <a href="/power-bi-quickstart">Power BI Quickstart</a>.
        If the model is already the thing people argue about, we work on that model first.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="who">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Who we serve</p>
      <h2 id="who">Who the work is for</h2>
      <p>The same model has to serve more than one meeting. These are the rooms we usually sit in.</p>
      <div class="cards">
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="finance" />
          <h3>Finance</h3>
          <p>The work is the close, forecast against actuals, and one profit-and-loss or cash figure leadership will sign. See <a href="/power-bi-for-finance">Power BI for finance</a> and <a href="/blog/excel-vs-power-bi-financial-reporting">Excel vs Power BI for financial reporting</a>.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="operations" />
          <h3>Operations</h3>
          <p>The work is throughput and schedule, with the shop floor and finance on one set of books. See <a href="/power-bi-for-manufacturing">Power BI for manufacturing</a>.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="sales" />
          <h3>Sales</h3>
          <p>The work is pipeline and bookings that finance will accept, instead of a forecast that never ties out. See <a href="/power-bi-for-sales">Power BI for sales</a> and <a href="/blog/sales-forecast-vs-finance-bookings">sales forecast vs finance bookings</a>.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="marketing" />
          <h3>Marketing</h3>
          <p>The work is spend tied to pipeline, using the same definitions sales and finance already use.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="it" />
          <h3>IT</h3>
          <p>The work is refresh, gateways, capacity, and a certified model, instead of a pile of reports nobody owns. Read <a href="/blog/unused-power-bi-licenses">unused Power BI licenses</a>.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="project" />
          <h3>Project management</h3>
          <p>The work is a backlog and a delivery date, so the model actually gets used. See <a href="/data-project-management-change-leadership">analytics project management</a>.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="supply" />
          <h3>Supply chain</h3>
          <p>Inventory, on-time delivery, and supplier numbers are in the same model as finance. See <a href="/power-bi-for-supply-chain">Power BI for supply chain</a>.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="hr" />
          <h3>HR</h3>
          <p>Headcount, labor, and people costs tie to the profit and loss. See <a href="/power-bi-for-hr">Power BI for HR</a>.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="quality" />
          <h3>Quality</h3>
          <p>Scrap, yield, and customer escapes are in the Monday pack, not only in a plant report. See <a href="/power-bi-for-quality">Power BI for quality</a>.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-alt band-quickstart" aria-labelledby="qs-band">
    <div class="wrap band-center">
      <h2 id="qs-band">Power BI Quickstart</h2>
      <p class="lede">It is one painful report, rebuilt in two weeks, with training and a week of support.</p>
      <p class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/power-bi-quickstart">See Power BI Quickstart</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="offerings">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Offerings</p>
      <h2 id="offerings">From a single report to a retainer</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        The work starts with the meeting that is stuck. Some companies need one report. Some need the order of work. Some need someone to keep the model honest after the project team leaves.
      </p>
      <div class="cards-2">
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="strategy" />
          <h3>Analytics strategy roadmap</h3>
          <p>We name the decisions the reporting has to support, the data those decisions depend on, and the requests that can wait.</p>
          <p><a href="/analytics-ai-strategy-roadmap">See the analytics strategy roadmap</a></p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="project" />
          <h3>Data and analytics project management</h3>
          <p>Someone owns scope, the calendar, and whether people can open the report before the meeting that needs it.</p>
          <p><a href="/data-project-management-change-leadership">See data and analytics project management</a></p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="dashboard" />
          <h3>Dashboard optimization</h3>
          <p>We look at the Power BI you already have: how long it takes to refresh, whether anyone opens it, and whether two reports disagree. Then we tighten the model and the page so people use it.</p>
          <p><a href="/power-bi-dashboard-optimization-ai-insights">See dashboard optimization</a></p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="retainer" />
          <h3>Managed advisory retainer</h3>
          <p>After go-live, definitions drift and copies appear. The retainer keeps one version current.</p>
          <p><a href="/managed-advisory-retainer">See the managed advisory retainer</a></p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="training" />
          <h3>Training</h3>
          <p>The session uses the model your team already paid for. People click through their own report, not a sample file.</p>
          <p><a href="/power-bi-training">See Power BI training</a></p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="conversational" />
          <h3>Conversational analytics</h3>
          <p>A plain question goes to a certified Power BI or Fabric model and comes back with a measure the meeting already trusts.</p>
          <p><a href="/conversational-analytics">See conversational analytics</a></p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="why">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 id="why">How we work</h2>
      <div class="cards">
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="focus" />
          <h3>The meeting comes before another report</h3>
          <p>Roadmap and model work are tied to a meeting that already happens. We do not add a report nobody has asked to use.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="scale" />
          <h3>The model still works after the next hire</h3>
          <p>There are shared definitions, a publish path, and a named owner, so the model does not depend on the first analyst who built it.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <LineIcon name="expertise" />
          <h3>People who still open Desktop</h3>
          <p>We build semantic models, refresh, and packs a controller can take into the close. We do not invent client logos. The work is meant to transfer to your team.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <article class="card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
        <LineIcon name="client" />
          <h3>Your systems and your calendar</h3>
          <p>The work uses ERP, Excel, and the Monday pack you already run. The scope is one painful report, or a longer engagement after a Session.</p>
        </article>
      <p><a href="/about">About Alluvium</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-alt" aria-labelledby="steps">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 id="steps">How an engagement usually starts</h2>
      <div class="steps">
        <article>
          <p class="step-num">01</p>
          <h3>Book a Session</h3>
          <p>We talk through the reporting problem and the meeting it has to serve.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="step-num">02</p>
          <h3>A roadmap or a defined package</h3>
          <p>We come back with a focused plan: what to do first, whether that is a cleanup, a Quickstart, or ongoing help.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="step-num">03</p>
          <h3>Build it with your people in the room</h3>
          <p>We implement with your team, and the test is whether the meeting uses the report.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand heading="Book a Session" text="Tell us the reporting pain and the meeting that matters. We will say whether a Quickstart, a Model Health Deep Dive, or a longer engagement fits." />
</BaseLayout>
